{
  "title": "Tuples and unpacking",
  "summary": "Fixed-size records, and taking things apart.",
  "minutes": 10,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Tuples",
      "body": [
        "A `tuple` is an **immutable** sequence, written with commas (the parentheses are often optional):",
        "",
        "```python",
        "point = (3, 4)",
        "x, y = point          # unpacking",
        "rgb = 255, 128, 0     # also a tuple",
        "single = (5,)         # one-element tuple needs the comma",
        "```",
        "",
        "Use tuples for small fixed groups where position has meaning, like C#'s `(int X, int Y)` value tuples. Because they are immutable they can be dictionary keys; lists cannot."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "mcq",
      "prompt": "Which of these is a tuple with one element?",
      "options": [
        "`(5,)`",
        "`(5)`",
        "`[5]`",
        "`tuple 5`"
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explain": "`(5)` is just the number 5 in parentheses. The comma makes the tuple."
    },
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Star unpacking",
      "body": [
        "Unpacking works on any iterable, and a `*name` soaks up the rest into a list:",
        "",
        "```python",
        "first, *middle, last = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]",
        "# first = 1, middle = [2, 3, 4], last = 5",
        "",
        "head, *tail = \"abc\"      # 'a', ['b', 'c']",
        "for name, (lat, lon) in [(\"Oslo\", (59.9, 10.7))]:",
        "    ...                   # nested unpacking",
        "```",
        "",
        "Functions return several values by returning a tuple: `return lo, hi`, then `lo, hi = bounds(xs)`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "first, *rest = [10, 20, 30]",
        "*init, last = \"hello\"",
        "a, (b, c) = 1, (2, 3)",
        "print(first, rest)",
        "print(init, last)",
        "print(a + b + c)"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "10 [20, 30]",
        "['h', 'e', 'l', 'l'] o",
        "6"
      ],
      "explain": "Starred names always get a list, even from a string."
    },
    {
      "type": "mcq",
      "prompt": "What happens: `a, b = [1, 2, 3]`?",
      "options": [
        "`ValueError: too many values to unpack`",
        "`a = 1, b = [2, 3]`",
        "`a = 1, b = 2` and 3 is dropped",
        "`a = [1, 2, 3]`, `b = None`"
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explain": "Plain unpacking needs an exact match. Use `a, *b = ...` to collect the extras."
    },
    {
      "type": "blanks",
      "prompt": "Split a log line into its parts.",
      "code": [
        "line = \"2024-05-01 ERROR disk full on sda\"",
        "date, level, __1__words = line.split()",
        "message = \" \".__2__(words)",
        "print(level, __3__)"
      ],
      "blanks": [
        "*",
        "join",
        "message"
      ],
      "bank": [
        "**",
        "split",
        "words",
        "&"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "sort",
      "prompt": "Mutable or immutable?",
      "buckets": [
        "Mutable (can change in place)",
        "Immutable"
      ],
      "items": [
        [
          "`list`",
          0
        ],
        [
          "`dict`",
          0
        ],
        [
          "`set`",
          0
        ],
        [
          "`tuple`",
          1
        ],
        [
          "`str`",
          1
        ],
        [
          "`int`",
          1
        ],
        [
          "`frozenset`",
          1
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "title": "Min, max and middle",
      "prompt": [
        "Read a line of whole numbers. Sort them, then use star-unpacking to print the smallest, the largest, and how many are in between:",
        "",
        "```text",
        "min 1 max 9 between 3",
        "```",
        "",
        "for `5 9 1 3 7`. The line always has at least two numbers."
      ],
      "starter": "nums = sorted(int(x) for x in input().split())\n",
      "solution": [
        "nums = sorted(int(x) for x in input().split())",
        "lo, *mid, hi = nums",
        "print(f\"min {lo} max {hi} between {len(mid)}\")"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "name": "five",
          "stdin": "5 9 1 3 7\n",
          "expect": "min 1 max 9 between 3"
        },
        {
          "name": "two",
          "stdin": "4 2\n",
          "expect": "min 2 max 4 between 0"
        },
        {
          "name": "negatives",
          "stdin": "-3 10 -8 0\n",
          "expect": "min -8 max 10 between 2"
        }
      ],
      "require": [
        {
          "pattern": "\\*\\s*\\w+\\s*,",
          "message": "Uses star unpacking"
        }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "`lo, *mid, hi = nums`"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
